  "body": "## What was broken\n\nThe failover badge and panel added in 4.35.0 have been **rendering unstyled on main since 4.35.1**. Six classes were emitted by `admin.js` with no CSS rule at all:\n\n```\nfallback-badge           css:0  js-emits:1\nfallback-route           css:0  js-emits:1\nfallback-route-path      css:0  js-emits:1\nfallback-route-arrow     css:0  js-emits:1\nfallback-route-count     css:0  js-emits:1\nfallback-route-served    css:0  js-emits:1\n```\n\n## How, because the mechanism is not obvious\n\nMy mistake. While landing 4.35.1 I hit a conflict in `admin.css` between the failover block and a reduced-motion block, **resolved it correctly by hand, verified both sides were present**, and then ran `git checkout --theirs .` to clear the conflicted state. That threw the resolution away and took the stashed side wholesale — the side that predated the failover CSS.\n\nThe commit that followed looked clean and CI was green.\n\n## The real problem\n\n**An unstyled class is not an error.** The page loads, the script runs, every test passes, and the only signal is a person looking at it. This surfaced purely because I ran a class cross-check by hand while rebasing the next branch — not because anything caught it.\n\nSo the fix is the restored CSS *plus* a test that makes this failure loud: every class `admin.js` emits under a feature prefix must have a rule in `admin.css`. Scoped to prefixes rather than every class in the file, so it stays a real assertion instead of a list nobody maintains.\n\n## The guard needed two passes of its own\n\nA naive `` [`\"]([^`\"]+)[`\"] `` capture stops at the first quote **inside** a template interpolation, and a nested template literal leaves an unterminated `${` behind. Both produced fragments that were never class names, which would have made the test noise that gets deleted. Now: template literals and plain strings are matched separately, complete interpolations are stripped, and a trailing unterminated one is cut — so it checks base classes and does not dictate how the script writes its interpolations.\n\n## Verification\n\nI checked the guard **actually catches the bug it exists for**, rather than only passing:\n\n```\n# rename .fallback-badge in the stylesheet\n$ pytest ...::test_admin_static_styles_every_class_the_script_emits\nAssertionError: emitted by admin.js with no CSS rule: ['fallback-badge']\n```\n\nRestored to green afterwards.\n\n```\n$ uv run ty check\nAll checks passed!\n\n$ env -u ENABLE_WEB_SERVER_TOOLS -u EXA_API_KEY uv run pytest tests/api/test_admin.py -q\n72 passed in 17.42s\n```\n\nBraces balance, and `.analytics-empty` is not duplicated — it survived on main, so only the genuinely lost rules were restored.\n\n## Residual risks\n\n- The guard covers class **names**, not whether a rule is correct or reachable. A rule that exists but is overridden still passes.\n- It is prefix-scoped (`fallback-`, `route-`, `get-started-`, `ws-`, `model-chain-`). A future feature using a new prefix is not covered until the prefix is added — deliberate, since the alternative is a whole-file check that fails on every shared utility class.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)",
