    "message": "fix(admin): restore the failover styles and stop this losing again\n\nThe failover badge and panel added in 4.35.0 have been rendering unstyled on\nmain since 4.35.1. Six classes -- fallback-badge, fallback-route,\nfallback-route-path, fallback-route-arrow, fallback-route-count and\nfallback-route-served -- were emitted by admin.js with no CSS rule at all.\n\nMy mistake, and worth writing down because the mechanism is not obvious. While\nlanding 4.35.1 I hit a conflict in admin.css between the failover block and a\nreduced-motion block, resolved it correctly by hand, verified both sides were\npresent, and then ran `git checkout --theirs .` to clear the conflicted state.\nThat threw the resolution away and took the stashed side wholesale -- the side\nthat predated the failover CSS. The commit that followed looked clean, CI was\ngreen, and nothing anywhere notices that a class has no rule.\n\nThat is the actual problem: an unstyled class is not an error. The page loads,\nthe script runs, every test passes, and the only signal is a person looking at\nit. It surfaced here purely because I ran a class cross-check by hand while\nrebasing the next branch.\n\nSo the fix is the restored CSS plus a test that makes the failure loud:\nevery class admin.js emits under a feature prefix must have a rule in\nadmin.css. Scoped to prefixes rather than every class in the file so it stays\na real assertion instead of a list nobody maintains.\n\nThe test needed two passes of its own to be worth having. A naive\n`[`\"]([^`\"]+)[`\"]` capture stops at the first quote *inside* a template\ninterpolation, and a nested template literal leaves an unterminated `${`\nbehind; both produced fragments that were never class names and would have\nmade the test noise. Template literals and plain strings are now matched\nseparately, complete interpolations are stripped, and a trailing unterminated\none is cut -- so the test checks base classes and does not dictate how the\nscript writes its interpolations.\n\nVerified the guard actually catches the bug it exists for, rather than only\npassing: renaming .fallback-badge in the stylesheet makes it fail and name\nthe class. Restored to green afterwards. 72 tests pass, ruff and ty clean,\nbraces balance, and .analytics-empty is not duplicated -- it survived on main\nand only the genuinely lost rules were restored.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>",
